    "section_summary": {
      "type": "string",
      "extract_value": "The section reports that pressure to eat messages were generally not associated with young adults' intuitive eating or disordered eating, though they were slightly associated with women's perceptions that their family pressured them to be lean and with women's BMI, and not related to eating or body-related variables for men. It discusses how caregiver pressure to eat nutrient-dense, high-satiety foods may lead young adults who gain independence to avoid those foods, potentially contributing to increased weight if replaced by high-calorie, low-satiety foods. The authors conclude these findings provide limited support for the construct validity of the PEM subscale.",
